                    <div class="content-content publication-content">
                        
                        
                            <h2 class="subheader">Abstract</h2>
                        <div class="rendering rendering_researchoutput  rendering_researchoutput_abstractportal rendering_contributiontojournal rendering_abstractportal rendering_contributiontojournal_abstractportal"><div class="textblock">Is palantir a ‘superweapon' or a marketing masterclass? This article examines the emergence of a ‘nationalist-militarist' faction within the contemporary tech oligarchy through a critical reading of The Technological Republic (2025) by Palantir CEO Alexander Karp and Nicholas Zamiska. I argue that the book operates as an ideological manifesto designed to re-legitimize Silicon Valley's entanglement with the U.S. national security state, framing this alliance as a patriotic necessity in a Schmittian struggle against China. Rejecting both the company's self-mythologizing and popular portrayals of Palantir as an all-powerful, shadowy monolith, the article draws on STS scholarship and Göran Therborn's theory of ruling-class reproduction to analyze the ‘Palantir imaginary.' This imaginary of seamless, illiberal control is not an empirical reality but a carefully branded sociotechnical fantasy, cultivated to align with security agencies' desire for total situational awareness. I show how Palantir positions itself not merely as a software provider but as a strategic political actor engaged in an intra-oligarchic struggle against ‘globalist’ consumer-tech firms, advancing a technocratic, anti-democratic vision of sovereignty that replaces democratic oversight with proprietary algorithmic governance. The article concludes by demonstrating how the fusion of corporate accumulation and state power into a black-boxed sociotechnical system poses a fundamental challenge to democratic accountability.</div></div>
                        <div class="rendering rendering_researchoutput  rendering_researchoutput_detailsportal rendering_contributiontojournal rendering_detailsportal rendering_contributiontojournal_detailsportal"><div class="article"><table class="properties"><tbody><tr class="language"><th scope="row">Originalsprog</th><td>Engelsk</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tidsskrift</th><td><a rel="Journal" href="#" class="link"><span>Science as Culture</span></a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Vol/bind</th><td>Latest articles</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Antal sider</th><td>11</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ISSN</th><td>0950-5431</td></tr><tr class="digital_object_identifiers"><th scope="row">DOI</th><td><ul class="relations digital_object_identifiers"><li><a onclick="window.open(this.href, '_blank','noopener,noreferrer'); return false;" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/09505431.2025.2607360" class="link"><span>https://doi.org/10.1080/09505431.2025.2607360</span></a> </li></ul></td></tr><tr class="status"><th scope="row">Status</th><td><span class="prefix">Udgivet - </span><span class="date">2026</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
                        

                        

                        

