            <div id="cite-RIS" class="tab " role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-5" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
                <div class="rendering rendering_researchoutput  rendering_researchoutput_ris rendering_contributiontojournal rendering_ris rendering_contributiontojournal_ris"><p>TY  - JOUR</p><p>T1  - Manufacturing the Leviathan</p><p>T2  - Palantir’s ‘Technological republic’ and the nationalist faction of the tech oligarchy</p><p>AU  - Taskale, Ali Riza</p><p>PY  - 2026</p><p>Y1  - 2026</p><p>N2  - Is palantir a ‘superweapon' or a marketing masterclass? This article examines the emergence of a ‘nationalist-militarist' faction within the contemporary tech oligarchy through a critical reading of The Technological Republic (2025) by Palantir CEO Alexander Karp and Nicholas Zamiska. I argue that the book operates as an ideological manifesto designed to re-legitimize Silicon Valley's entanglement with the U.S. national security state, framing this alliance as a patriotic necessity in a Schmittian struggle against China. Rejecting both the company's self-mythologizing and popular portrayals of Palantir as an all-powerful, shadowy monolith, the article draws on STS scholarship and Göran Therborn's theory of ruling-class reproduction to analyze the ‘Palantir imaginary.' This imaginary of seamless, illiberal control is not an empirical reality but a carefully branded sociotechnical fantasy, cultivated to align with security agencies' desire for total situational awareness. I show how Palantir positions itself not merely as a software provider but as a strategic political actor engaged in an intra-oligarchic struggle against ‘globalist’ consumer-tech firms, advancing a technocratic, anti-democratic vision of sovereignty that replaces democratic oversight with proprietary algorithmic governance. The article concludes by demonstrating how the fusion of corporate accumulation and state power into a black-boxed sociotechnical system poses a fundamental challenge to democratic accountability.</p><p>AB  - Is palantir a ‘superweapon' or a marketing masterclass? This article examines the emergence of a ‘nationalist-militarist' faction within the contemporary tech oligarchy through a critical reading of The Technological Republic (2025) by Palantir CEO Alexander Karp and Nicholas Zamiska. I argue that the book operates as an ideological manifesto designed to re-legitimize Silicon Valley's entanglement with the U.S. national security state, framing this alliance as a patriotic necessity in a Schmittian struggle against China. Rejecting both the company's self-mythologizing and popular portrayals of Palantir as an all-powerful, shadowy monolith, the article draws on STS scholarship and Göran Therborn's theory of ruling-class reproduction to analyze the ‘Palantir imaginary.' This imaginary of seamless, illiberal control is not an empirical reality but a carefully branded sociotechnical fantasy, cultivated to align with security agencies' desire for total situational awareness. I show how Palantir positions itself not merely as a software provider but as a strategic political actor engaged in an intra-oligarchic struggle against ‘globalist’ consumer-tech firms, advancing a technocratic, anti-democratic vision of sovereignty that replaces democratic oversight with proprietary algorithmic governance. The article concludes by demonstrating how the fusion of corporate accumulation and state power into a black-boxed sociotechnical system poses a fundamental challenge to democratic accountability.</p><p>KW  - Tech oligarchy</p><p>KW  - The Technological Republic</p><p>KW  - Palantir imaginary</p><p>KW  - Schmittian geopolitics</p><p>KW  - Algorithmic governance</p><p>KW  - Anti-democratic turn</p><p>KW  - Tech oligarchy</p><p>KW  - The Technological Republic</p><p>KW  - Palantir imaginary</p><p>KW  - Schmittian geopolitics</p><p>KW  - Algorithmic governance</p><p>KW  - Anti-democratic turn</p><p>U2  - 10.1080/09505431.2025.2607360</p><p>DO  - 10.1080/09505431.2025.2607360</p><p>M3  - Journal article</p><p>SN  - 0950-5431</p><p>VL  - Latest articles</p><p>JO  - Science as Culture</p><p>JF  - Science as Culture</p><p>ER  - </p></div>
